From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: turbinebmw <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 19:30:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] input/battery: Suspend HID devices and pause battery
 notifications over system sleep

bluetoothd is not told about system sleep: the HoG input plugin has
suspend/resume callbacks that write the HID Control Point, but the only
backend is suspend-none. A keyboard kept connected over suspend so that it
can wake the system (Surface Pro Flex Keyboard: HID Information flags
RemoteWake | NormallyConnectable) then sends a Battery Level notification
about every 62 s, each of which wakes the host.

Add src/sleep.c: it watches systemd-logind's PrepareForSleep and holds a
delay inhibitor, running registered suspend callbacks and releasing the
inhibitor 300 ms later so their writes reach devices first; on resume it
runs the resume callbacks and takes the inhibitor again. Use it for:

- HoG: suspend-sleep replaces suspend-none, so HID Control Point Suspend
  (0x00) / Exit Suspend (0x01) are written on sleep and resume. The write
  now covers every HID service instance of a device, not just the first,
  and skips bonded devices that aren't connected (no HoG instance; the
  callback dereferenced NULL for them, unnoticed while it never ran).
- HoG: input reports in vendor-defined top-level collections (usage page
  0xff00-0xffff, found by walking the report map) have their notifications
  turned off over sleep and back on after it. The Flex Keyboard ignores
  HID Suspend for its vendor status report (ID 0xd1, page 0xfff5), which
  otherwise woke the host about a minute into every suspend.
- Battery: Battery Level notifications are unregistered (CCC off) on sleep
  and registered again on resume.
---
